# Bible.Alarm test architecture

## Repo layout — where do tests live?

All 10 test projects live in a flat `tests/` directory at the repo root, and the solution lives at the repo root too:

```
Bible.Alarm.sln                                  # solution file (repo root)
src/                                             # production app + shared lib
.tools/                                          # CLI dev tools (cataloger, db migration, etc.)
libraries/                                       # vendored / forked libraries
tests/
├── Platforms/
│   ├── Android/                                 # Android-only smoke tests (compiled by Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android only)
│   └── iOS/                                     # iOS-only smoke tests (compiled by Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS only)
├── Bible.Alarm.Tests/                           # cross-platform xunit fixtures + Platforms/Windows/ host-only
├── Bible.Alarm.Shared.Tests/                    # net10.0 tests over Bible.Alarm.Shared (no MAUI)
├── Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android/                   # device-test host (.apk launched as Activity via adb)
├── Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS/                       # device-test host (.app via xharness apple test)
├── Bible.Alarm.UITests/                         # Appium UI integration tests (Windows/Android/iOS) — see UITESTS.md
├── Bible.Alarm.Cataloger.Tests/                 # tools/ test
├── Bible.Alarm.DbMigration.Tests/               #
├── Bible.Alarm.IosScreenshotResizer.Tests/      #
├── Bible.Alarm.VersionPatcher.Tests/            #
├── CommunityToolkit.Maui.MediaElement.Tests/    # libraries/ test
├── README.md                                    # local-dev workflow (host + device unit tests)
├── UITESTS.md                                   # local-dev workflow (Appium UI tests + install steps)
├── run-tests.ps1                                # local orchestrator (Windows + Android + iOS over SSH)
└── run-ui-tests.ps1                             # local orchestrator (Appium-driven UI tests, all 3 platforms)
```

**On disk: flat. In the IDE: bucketed.** The .sln defines five nested SolutionFolders so the IDE tree shows `tests / { app, tools, libraries, ui }`:

- `tests / app`   → Bible.Alarm.Tests, Bible.Alarm.Shared.Tests, Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android, Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS
- `tests / tools` → Cataloger.Tests, DbMigration.Tests, IosScreenshotResizer.Tests, VersionPatcher.Tests
- `tests / libraries` → CommunityToolkit.Maui.MediaElement.Tests
- `tests / ui` → Bible.Alarm.UITests

If you `dotnet sln Bible.Alarm.sln list` you should see exactly **17 csprojs** (7 production + 10 tests). Anything else means a project moved or a path drifted.

## Status snapshot — what already exists (do NOT recreate)

Last verified on `develop`:

- All 10 test projects sit under `tests/`, the .sln is at the repo root, IDE folders nest correctly.
- `dotnet sln Bible.Alarm.sln list` returns 17 csprojs.
- `dotnet test Bible.Alarm.sln --filter "Platform!=Android&Platform!=iOS&UI!=Windows&UI!=Android&UI!=iOS"` runs 1,899 tests in 7 host-based projects, exit 0. (The four trait clauses are load-bearing — see [Hard rules](#hard-rules--do-not-break) #10.)
- `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android` builds for `net10.0-android` and runs as an **Activity** (`tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android/Platforms/Android/TestRunnerActivity.cs`) launched via `adb shell am start`. It uses `Microsoft.DotNet.XHarness.DefaultAndroidEntryPoint.Xunit` *as a managed library only* — `xharness android test` is **not** in the invocation path because Android's custom `Instrumentation` class is constructed before Mono initializes (see [Why no Instrumentation on Android](#why-no-instrumentation-on-android)).
- `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS` builds for `net10.0-ios` and is wired to xharness with `Microsoft.DotNet.XHarness.TestRunners.Xunit`. iOS goes through `xharness apple test` (the XCUITest entry-point path runs after the managed runtime is up, so it is unaffected by the Android race).
- `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests` builds for `net10.0` and uses `Appium.WebDriver` 8.2.0. Three smoke tests (one per platform), each tagged `[Trait("UI","<platform>")]`. They never run in the coverage pass — see the dedicated section below.
- One platform-only smoke test exists per device-test host (do **not** duplicate them — extend or replace if the production type they touch ever moves):
  - Android: `tests/Platforms/Android/NotificationPermissionHelperSmokeTests.cs` exercises `Bible.Alarm.Platforms.Android.Services.Helpers.NotificationPermissionHelper`.
  - iOS: `tests/Platforms/iOS/IosNotificationPermissionServiceSmokeTests.cs` exercises `Bible.Alarm.Platforms.iOS.Services.Helpers.IosNotificationPermissionService`.
- One Appium UI smoke test exists per platform (`tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/Smoke/{Windows,Android,Ios}HomePageUITests.cs`) — they all assert the home page renders by waiting for `MobileBy.AccessibilityId("HomePage")`. Do **not** add more UI tests until the corresponding `ui-*` CI job is wired up; flaky UI tests with no gate quickly bit-rot.
- The CI fan-out for *coverage* is `test-windows`, `test-android`, `test-ios` → `sonar` (single SonarCloud analysis with merged OpenCover XML). UI tests are NOT part of this fan-out and do not contribute coverage.

If you came here to "add an Android/iOS test that proves the runner works", that's already done — write coverage tests instead, following the workflow below.

## If you're here to improve coverage — agent workflow

Follow these steps in order. Do **not** push to CI before step 5 passes locally; the Android/iOS jobs are 10–20 min each and CI minutes are a finite resource.

1. **Locate the gap.** Get the latest OpenCover/Cobertura report. Two ways:
   - From CI artifacts: download `coverage-windows`, `coverage-android`, `coverage-ios` from the most recent green Actions run, drop them in `TestResults/coverage-{windows,android,ios}/`, then merge:
     ```pwsh
     dotnet tool restore
     dotnet reportgenerator `
         "-reports:TestResults/coverage-windows/coverage-windows.xml;TestResults/coverage-android/coverage-android.xml;TestResults/coverage-ios/coverage-ios.xml" `
         "-targetdir:TestResults/merged" `
         -reporttypes:Html
     start TestResults/merged/index.html
     ```
   - Or run locally end-to-end: `./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform All -MacHost user@mac.local`. Same merge step happens automatically; report at `TestResults/merged/index.html`.
   - Sort by **uncovered LOC × file weight** (biggest gains first). In the OpenCover XML, `<SequencePoint vc="0" ... />` is an uncovered line; `<Method ... visited="false">` is an entirely uncovered method.
2. **Pick a target file.** Match it against the placement table in [Where does a new test go?](#where-does-a-new-test-go). The decision drives which test project, folder, and `[Trait]` you need.
3. **Find the closest exemplar.** See [Quick-reference paths to known-good exemplars](#quick-reference-paths-to-known-good-exemplars). Copy structure, naming, and assertion style.
4. **Write the test.** Mirror the production folder, follow the [xunit conventions](#test-fixture-style-xunit-conventions-used-in-this-repo). One assertion idea per `[Fact]`; no `Task.Delay`; no real I/O.
5. **Validate locally** (the platform-specific paths below). Coverage XML must regenerate and show the new lines covered. `dotnet test` exit code must be `0`. Do not skip this even for "trivial" tests — the device-test hosts have non-obvious build interactions documented under [Hard rules](#hard-rules--do-not-break).
6. **Commit and push.** CI will rerun all three platforms in parallel and feed the merged coverage to SonarCloud automatically (`sonar` job).

If a step fails, do **not** loosen `[Trait]`, the Compile glob excludes, or the SonarScanner exclusions to "make it green". Those are load-bearing — fix the underlying issue or escalate.

## Pre-flight — what your dev box needs before you can run platform tests locally

| Platform | Required tools                                                                                  | One-time setup                                                                                                                                          |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Windows  | .NET SDK matching `global.json` (10.0.202 today). PowerShell 7+. Visual Studio MAUI workload.   | `dotnet workload install maui`                                                                                                                          |
| Android  | All of Windows + Android Studio SDK Platform-Tools (`adb`, `emulator`) on `PATH` *or* `$env:ANDROID_HOME` set. At least one AVD (API 30+). | `dotnet workload install maui-android`. Create an AVD in Android Studio (Pixel 8, API 34 works). No xharness needed locally for Android — `tests/run-tests.ps1` drives the run with plain `adb`. |
| iOS      | A reachable Mac on the LAN with passwordless SSH, .NET 10 SDK, `maui-ios` workload, Xcode 16+ with iOS 18 simulator runtime. | On the Mac: `dotnet workload install maui-ios`, install Xcode + simulator runtime. On Windows: `ssh-keygen` + copy key to Mac. Confirm with `ssh user@mac.local 'dotnet --info'`. |

Verify before running `tests/run-tests.ps1`:

```pwsh
# Sanity checks the script itself does not perform up-front
dotnet --info | Select-String -Pattern '^\s*Version:'        # matches global.json
adb devices                                                  # only matters for Android
ssh user@mac.local 'xcrun simctl list runtimes | head -2'    # only matters for iOS
```

If any check fails, fix the prerequisite before retrying — the script's error messages assume the workload is installed and the host is reachable.

## Validate locally — exact commands per platform

Pick the **smallest** scope that covers your change, not "All", to keep the inner loop fast.

```pwsh
# After editing a cross-platform fixture under tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/ (no platform-only deps)
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform Windows
# ~30s on a warm tree. Coverage at TestResults/windows/coverage.windows.opencover.xml.

# After editing tests/Platforms/Android/** or any production code under src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Android/**
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform Android -AndroidAvd <your-avd-name>
# ~3-5 min cold (AVD boot + APK build + adb install + Activity launch + done.txt poll).
# Coverage at TestResults/android/coverage.android.opencover.xml (pulled off /sdcard/Documents/test-results/).

# After editing tests/Platforms/iOS/** or any production code under src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/iOS/**
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform iOS -MacHost user@mac.local
# ~5-8 min (rsync + simulator boot + build + xharness). Coverage at TestResults/ios/coverage.ios.opencover.xml.

# Pre-push smoke (only when changes span platforms or you've touched the test infrastructure itself)
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform All -MacHost user@mac.local
```

What "passing" means before you push:

1. Script exits with `Done.` in green and exit code 0 (`$LASTEXITCODE` is 0).
2. The relevant `coverage.<platform>.opencover.xml` exists and is non-empty.
3. The new lines you intended to cover show `vc="1"` (or higher) in that XML, *not* `vc="0"`. If they're still `vc="0"`, your test isn't actually exercising the path — fix the test, don't merge.
4. No build warnings introduced (`-Verbose` to surface them).

Skipping the merge for fast iteration: `-SkipMerge` avoids the ReportGenerator step but keeps the per-platform XML.

## Diagnosing a failing CI job (matrix → fix path)

| Failure                                                                              | First place to look                                                                                                  | Likely fix                                                                                       |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `test-windows`: `error CS0246: The type or namespace 'Bible.Alarm.Platforms.Android.*'` | A platform-only test file got added under `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/` outside `Platforms/Windows/`.                  | Move it to `tests/Platforms/{Android,iOS}/` and add the matching `[Trait("Platform","...")]`.    |
| `test-android`: `XAGMM7009: There can be only one type with an [Application] attribute` | Someone added a `MainApplication.cs`/`MauiProgram.cs` to `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android`.                              | Delete it. The test host inherits Bible.Alarm's `MainApplication` via project ref by design.     |
| `test-android`: `java.lang.UnsatisfiedLinkError: No implementation found for void ...TestInstrumentation.n_onCreate(android.os.Bundle)` and the test process exits in ~5s with no test output. | Someone re-introduced an `[Instrumentation]`-attributed class (see [hard rules](#hard-rules--do-not-break) #5). Mono's JNI bindings are not registered before Android calls `Instrumentation.onCreate`. | Delete the `[Instrumentation]` class. Tests run via `TestRunnerActivity` launched with `adb shell am start -n com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests/.TestRunnerActivity`; do not add `<instrumentation>` back to `AndroidManifest.xml`. |
| `test-android` (`reactivecircus/android-emulator-runner`): `set: Illegal option -o pipefail` / exit code 2 right after "Emulator booted". | The action runs each `script:` **line** with `/usr/bin/sh -c` on `ubuntu-latest`; `sh` is **dash**, which does not implement bash's `pipefail`. | Do not use `set -euo pipefail` in an inline multi-line `script:` block. Prefer a single-line invoker: `bash "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE/.github/scripts/ci-android-device-tests.sh"` (see repo). |
| `test-android`: `No signed APK produced` immediately after emulator boot despite a green `Build Android test APK` step. | `reactivecircus/android-emulator-runner` parses `script:` with `lib/script-parser.js`: it **splits on newlines** and runs **each** non-`#` line as its **own** `sh -c '…'`. `APK=$(find …)` runs in process A; `if [ -z "$APK" ]` runs in process B where `APK` is empty. Comments are dropped entirely, but every executable line is isolated. | Never rely on multi-line shell state in the YAML `script:` block. Keep the full adb/TestRunnerActivity flow in **one** checked-in script (`.github/scripts/ci-android-device-tests.sh`) and pass exactly **one** line to `script:` so `parseScript` returns a single entry. |
| `test-android`: orchestrator times out after 15 minutes waiting for `done.txt`.       | The activity crashed before the `finally` block, or the device has no write access to `/sdcard/Documents/test-results/`. | Pull the run's `android-test-logs` artifact (or `TestResults/android/logcat.log` locally) and look for `FATAL EXCEPTION`. If the dir was owned by a previous-install UID, the orchestrator's `adb shell rm -rf` step is being skipped — make sure it runs before `am start`. |
| `test-android`: `coverage-android` artifact is uploaded but the file is 0 bytes.     | `Environment.Exit` is not firing coverlet's `AppDomain.ProcessExit` hook (rare). | `TestRunnerActivity.FlushCoverletTrackers` already invokes the trackers manually before exit, so the most common cause is that **no trackers were ever injected** at build time. See the next row. |
| `test-android` build: warning `[coverlet] Unable to instrument module: Bible.Alarm.dll … AssemblyResolutionException for 'Microsoft.Maui.Controls'` and **no `Coverlet.Core.Instrumentation.Tracker.*` strings inside `lib/x86_64/lib_Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.dll.so` after build**. | coverlet's Cecil resolver cannot find MAUI workload assemblies. CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies=true / PreserveCompilationContext=true fix the resolver but break the Android packaging (`XAAMP7004: An item with the same key has already been added. Key: Microsoft.CSharp`). | Currently a **known gap**: Android slice ships TestResults.xml + done.txt but no `coverage.android.opencover.xml`. Sonar still treats the line counts from Windows + iOS slices as authoritative and the build stays green (no failure on missing coverage). Long-term fix is to switch the device-test hosts to `coverlet.collector` and drive it via a manual XHarness-style invocation so the resolver runs in a vstest harness. Track in the open-issue list before re-attempting. |
| `test-android`: orchestrator pulls `TestResults\android\TestResults.xml\TestResults.xml` (xunit XML inside a same-named directory). | xharness's `DefaultAndroidEntryPoint` writes its results-file path as a **directory** containing the actual xunit XML inside it, even though every documented contract treats the path as a single file. | Both `tests/run-tests.ps1` (`Run-AndroidTests`) and `.github/workflows/build.yml` (`test-android`) run a defensive flatten after `adb pull` that promotes the inner `TestResults.xml` up one level, so downstream consumers always see a regular XML file. We deliberately do **not** flatten on-device: every write-after-delete pattern on `/sdcard` (FUSE-backed MediaStore) intermittently raises ENOENT or EBUSY because the MediaProvider's caches lag the underlying filesystem, leaving stragglers like `TestResults.xml.__tmp`. Stick with host-side flatten. |
| `run-ui-tests.ps1 -Platform Windows`: deploy fails with `WINAPPSDKGENERATEPROJECTPRIFILE PRI252: 0xdef00071 - File ...win-x64\CommunityToolkit.Maui.MediaElement.pri not found`. | Bible.Alarm's MSIX deploy looks for transitive .pri resources at `bin/<config>/<tfm>/<rid>/<asm>.pri`. The `<Properties>` metadata on Bible.Alarm's ProjectReference to MediaElement REPLACES MSBuild's default global-property propagation, so RuntimeIdentifier never reaches the dependent project and MediaElement builds without the RID-suffixed output path (Debug only — Release works because the vendored MediaElement csproj has a Release-only `AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath=true` shim). | Pre-existing infrastructure issue, unrelated to the Android Activity-runner refactor. The clean fix needs both edges in lock-step: change `<Properties>` to `<AdditionalProperties>` on Bible.Alarm's ProjectReference *and* widen MediaElement's `AppendRuntimeIdentifierToOutputPath` shim to non-Release. We tried this once and ran into APPX1101 (duplicate .pdb at the non-RID and RID paths) — a sign that MAUI Windows + Bible.Alarm's specific Properties forwarding produces a build cycle that lands the same library twice. Track as a follow-up; the production deploy still works in Release, only the Debug UI test orchestrator is blocked. |
| Mac dev box: `dotnet: command not found` after running anything that did `rm -rf "~"` (or any other tilde-quoted path). | macOS zsh expands `~` even inside double quotes by default — the literal `~` directory created by an earlier mistake (`mkdir -p '~/path'`, single-quoted, which preserved the literal) gets nuked along with `$HOME/.dotnet`, `$HOME/.npm`, `$HOME/.appium`, etc. The macOS sandbox protects `Music`/`Pictures`/`Desktop`/`Library` so they survive, masking how much else got wiped. | **Hard rule:** never write `rm -rf "~"` (or `rm -rf '~'`) — both forms tilde-expand under zsh. Use absolute paths like `rm -rf /literal/path/to/file`. To recover a wiped Mac: reinstall .NET 10 SDK (`./dotnet-install.sh --version $(cat global.json \| jq -r '.sdk.version')`), `dotnet workload install maui-ios`, `dotnet tool restore` (picks up xharness from `.config/dotnet-tools.json`). `tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform iOS` resolves `~/work/bible-alarm` via `ssh user@host "echo ~/path"` before invoking `mkdir`/`scp`, so the orchestrator itself never has the tilde-expansion ambiguity. |
| `tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform iOS`: bash on the Mac aborts with `set: pipefail: invalid option name`. | The PowerShell here-string the orchestrator passes to ssh has CRLF line endings, and macOS bash treats the trailing `\r` as part of the option name (`set -euo pipefail\r`). | The orchestrator already normalises CRLF→LF and then base64-encodes the script before piping into `bash -l` on the Mac. If you re-introduce a literal multi-line ssh argument (instead of the base64 round-trip), embedded newlines collapse and bash sees the whole script on line 0. Keep the base64 transport. |
| Android cross-platform suite: 14 pre-existing test failures surface now that the Activity-based runner actually executes them. | Tests in `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/` were authored against the Windows host's runtime/theme/dispatch ordering and carry no `[Trait("Platform","Windows")]` to gate them. Examples: `ThemeColorsTests.GetCurrentTheme_defaults_to_light_when_application_missing` (Android system theme is Dark), `NumberOfTrackStateInitializerTests.TryInitialize_maps_schedule_fields_for_windows_tfm` (the test name itself betrays Windows-only intent), `HomeViewModelFloatingButtonHandlerTests.ComputeFloatingButtonVisible_is_false_off_Android` (asserts the off-Android branch but runs ON Android), `AlarmSettingsContainerViewModelTests` × 3 (the Android dispatch ordering emits an extra `ContainerReadyAction`), `AlarmServiceTests` × 3 (Android scheduler short-circuits the inner mock), `AppLifecycleServiceTests.ReconcilePlaybackState_*` (Android also fires `SetCarPlayScreenAction`), `ResourceLoaderTests.GetFileInfo_*` (path-separator difference), `ArtworkManagerTests.UpdateArtwork_force_reload_*` (Android async timing), `SelectableTextColorConverterTests.Convert_without_application_resources_returns_purple_fallback` (different MAUI default purple), `ToastMiniBarInsetHelperTests.GetBottomInsetDip_*` (Android default inset = 96). | These are **legitimate test bugs** that the Activity runner exposed by actually running the suite on-device. Fix one fixture at a time — either (a) tag the test with `[Trait("Platform","Windows")]` and move it under `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Platforms/Windows/`, (b) split the assertion into per-platform variants, or (c) inject a `RuntimePlatform` abstraction so the production code under test exposes consistent behaviour. Do not paper over with `[Fact(Skip=...)]` without a tracking issue — the failures are real signals. |
| `test-ios` build: `ILLINK : error MT0180` / `MT2301` / `NETSDK1144: Optimizing assemblies for size failed` — "This version of Microsoft.iOS requires the iOS 26.0 SDK (shipped with Xcode 26.0) … set the managed linker behaviour to Link Framework SDKs Only". | GitHub `macos-latest` still ships Xcode 16.x; the .NET 10 `Microsoft.iOS` workload's default managed linker pass expects Xcode 26 SDK headers. The failure can surface on **`src/Bible.Alarm/Bible.Alarm.csproj`** *or* **`tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj`** — each is its own executable and runs the linker independently. Fixing only the production csproj is not enough for the device-test host build. | (1) `src/Bible.Alarm/Bible.Alarm.csproj`: `<MtouchLink>SdkOnly</MtouchLink>` when `GITHUB_ACTIONS` is `true`. (2) `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj`: same conditional PropertyGroup. (3) `.github/workflows/build.yml` `Build iOS test bundle` passes `-p:MtouchLink=SdkOnly` so transitive `net10.0-ios` projects (e.g. MediaElement) inherit it during that invocation. Remove once `macos-latest` ships Xcode 26+. |
| `test-ios`: app crashes immediately at launch with `NSObject ... already registered`  | A second `[Register("AppDelegate")]` slipped into the test bundle.                                                   | Make sure the test AppDelegate uses `[Register("BibleAlarmTestsAppDelegate")]`.                 |
| `test-ios` (`Run xharness on simulator`): `Run "dotnet tool restore" to make the "xharness" command available.` | The repo has `.config/dotnet-tools.json` registering `Microsoft.DotNet.XHarness.CLI` as a **local** tool. `dotnet xharness` resolves through that manifest before global tools; until `dotnet tool restore` runs from the repo root, the shim is missing. A prior `dotnet tool install --global` step does **not** satisfy local resolution. | Run `dotnet tool restore` in the same job (before `dotnet xharness apple test`) on macOS CI, or invoke `$HOME/.dotnet/tools/xharness` explicitly if you intentionally avoid the manifest. `.github/workflows/build.yml` restores before xharness. |
| `test-android`/`test-ios`: build fails with `NU1100: package not found Microsoft.DotNet.XHarness.*` | `NuGet.config` was overwritten or the `dotnet-eng` source / `packageSourceMapping` was removed.                | Restore `NuGet.config` from `git` — the `dotnet-eng` feed is mandatory.                          |
| `sonar`: SonarScanner reports 0% coverage even after green tests                     | Either an artifact name drifted (`coverage-windows.xml` etc.), or `sonar.cs.opencover.reportsPaths` lost a path.   | Check `actions/upload-artifact` `name:` matches `actions/download-artifact` `pattern:` (`coverage-*`) and the three paths in `sonar.cs.opencover.reportsPaths`. |
| `test-android`: `error MSB4018: GenerateRuntimeConfigurationFiles … android-x64/runtimeconfig.json directory not found` | `UndefineProperties="RuntimeIdentifier;RuntimeIdentifiers"` was removed from the `<ProjectReference>` to `Bible.Alarm.csproj`. | Restore it; without the strip, the test-host RID propagates and trips MAUI's runtimeconfig task. |
| `test-windows` only: `error CS0122: 'XYZ' is inaccessible due to its protection level` | A new `internal` consumer references a type but the test project isn't in `InternalsVisibleTo`.                      | Add the test project name to `<InternalsVisibleTo Include="..." />` in `Bible.Alarm.csproj`.     |
| `test-windows`: `Testhost process for source(s) '...Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.dll' exited with error: You must install or update .NET to run this application.` | Someone removed `<IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>` from `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj` / `Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj`. | Restore it from `git`. The device-test hosts must opt out of vstest discovery; they run via the adb/Activity flow (Android) or xharness (iOS). |
| Any job: `error MSB1009: Project file does not exist` on `src/Bible.Alarm.sln` or `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/...` | A workflow or script still has the pre-consolidation path. | Search the affected file for `src/Bible.Alarm.sln` / `src/Bible.Alarm.Tests*` / `.tools/Bible.Alarm.*.Tests` — they should all be `Bible.Alarm.sln` / `tests/Bible.Alarm.*` now. |
| `test-windows` / `Run-WindowsTests`: 3 unexpected failures with `Driver not initialised` or `BIBLE_ALARM_AUMID env var is not set`. | The coverage-pass `--filter` lost a `UI!=...` clause and pulled the Appium UI tests in. | Restore `Platform!=Android&Platform!=iOS&UI!=Windows&UI!=Android&UI!=iOS` in both `tests/run-tests.ps1` and `.github/workflows/build.yml`. UI tests must be opted into via `tests/run-ui-tests.ps1`, never the coverage pass. |
| `ui-windows` (when wired): `Could not discover Bible Alarm AUMID via Get-StartApps` | Production MSIX wasn't deployed before the test run, OR Developer Mode is disabled on the runner. | Ensure the `dotnet build … -t:Build,Deploy` step runs first, then sanity-check `Get-StartApps \| ? Name -eq 'Bible Alarm'` returns a row before invoking `dotnet test`. |
| `ui-windows` (when wired): WinAppDriver reports `WindowsApplicationDriver did not start` | Either WinAppDriver isn't installed, or the Appium `windows` driver never installed (`appium driver list --installed` doesn't show `windows`). | Re-run `appium driver install --source=npm appium-windows-driver` and confirm `WinAppDriver.exe` is present at `C:\Program Files (x86)\Windows Application Driver\` (or installed via winget). |
| `test-android` build: `Module test path '...bin/Release/net10.0-android/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.dll' not found` from `coverlet.msbuild`'s `SourceRootTranslator..ctor`. | Someone changed `_BibleAlarmHookCoverletInstrumentation` (in `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj`) to fire `AfterTargets="CoreCompile"` again. After CoreCompile, the test DLL only exists in `obj/`; coverlet reads `$(TargetPath)`, which resolves to `bin/<config>/<tfm>/<asm>.dll`. Locally this often fakes-out as success because previous incremental builds left a stale DLL in `bin/`. | Move the hook back to `AfterTargets="CopyFilesToOutputDirectory"` — that target is the standard MSBuild step that copies the freshly built DLL from `obj/` into `bin/`, after which `$(TargetPath)` resolves to a real on-disk file. The hook still runs before the IL linker / Android packaging consumes the DLL, so coverlet's instrumentation is preserved into `lib_*.dll.so`. |
| `test-ios` build: `error : iOS code signing key 'Apple Development: Created via API (...)' not found in keychain. [.../src/Bible.Alarm/Bible.Alarm.csproj::TargetFramework=net10.0-ios]` | `Bible.Alarm.csproj` declares `<CodesignKey>` for any iOS build, including simulator. CI builds the iOS test slice with `-p:RuntimeIdentifier=iossimulator-arm64`, but `Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj`'s ProjectReference *strips* RuntimeIdentifier via `UndefineProperties` (to dodge the per-RID `runtimeconfig.json` directory issue), so the inner build of Bible.Alarm sees an empty RID — the simulator-skip condition (which keys off `RuntimeIdentifier.StartsWith('iossimulator')`) falls through and codesign runs. | Keep two things in lock-step: (1) the gated `<CodesignKey>` PropertyGroup in `src/Bible.Alarm/Bible.Alarm.csproj` (`'$(TargetFramework)'=='net10.0-ios' AND !$([System.String]::Copy('$(RuntimeIdentifier)').StartsWith('iossimulator'))`) for IDE/dev-mode flows where RID is set on the executable build, AND (2) `AdditionalProperties="EnableCodeSigning=false"` on the iOS test's `<ProjectReference>` to `Bible.Alarm.csproj`, which forces the transitive build (with RID stripped) to skip signing. Test bundles never need signing — they only ever target the simulator — so this dual gate is correct. Do **not** unconditionally set `<CodesignKey>` — that breaks every CI run and any local simulator build. |
| `test-android` or `test-ios` build: `error MSB3371: The file "obj/Release/<tfm>/XamlC.stamp" cannot be created. The process cannot access the file '...XamlC.stamp' because it is being used by another process. [...MediaElement.csproj]` (or any project that uses MAUI XAML compilation). | MAUI's `XamlCTask` (Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Build.Tasks 10.0.41) writes a stamp file in `obj/` to mark XAML compilation done; on parallel MSBuild runs (the `dotnet build` default `-m`) two threads occasionally collide on the same project/TFM and fail the build. Linux runners hit this almost every run. See [dotnet/maui#12112](https://github.com/dotnet/maui/issues/12112). | Pass `-m:1` to `dotnet build` for the device-test-host APK/.app builds (already wired in `tests/run-tests.ps1` and both `test-android`/`test-ios` jobs in `.github/workflows/build.yml`). The cost is ~10s extra on a 2-min build; not removing it without a confirmed MAUI-side fix. Do **not** apply `-m:1` to the host-based pass (`Run-WindowsTests` / `test-windows`) — it's not affected by the race, and Windows benefits significantly from parallel build. |

On any failure, download the job's `android-test-logs` (Android) or `xharness-ios-logs` (iOS) artifact — they contain the on-device stdout / `logcat` tail / `error.txt` that the GitHub Actions log truncates.

