Total host-based pass on a clean Windows build today: **~1,899 tests** in 7 projects (the Android count is reported only after `TestRunnerActivity` writes `TestResults.xml` on the device; the iOS count is reported by `xharness apple test`. Neither is part of `dotnet test Bible.Alarm.sln`). If your local count drops below this without you having intentionally deleted tests, something glitched — check the `Compile` glob in the device-test hosts and the `--filter` in `tests/run-tests.ps1`.

### How shared code is wired without duplication

Cross-platform tests are physically owned by `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/`. The Android and iOS *device-test hosts* don't duplicate that source — they include it via:

```xml
<Compile Include="..\Bible.Alarm.Tests\**\*.cs"
         Exclude="..\Bible.Alarm.Tests\bin\**;..\Bible.Alarm.Tests\obj\**;..\Bible.Alarm.Tests\Platforms\**" />
```

The path is a **sibling reference** because all four `Bible.Alarm*.Tests*` projects now live next to each other under `tests/`. The `Platforms/**` exclusion is what keeps Windows-only fixtures (e.g. `WindowsToastXmlFactoryTests.cs`) out of the Android/iOS APK/.app — those types reference `Microsoft.UI.Xaml.*` which doesn't compile against `net10.0-android`.

Each device-test host references `Bible.Alarm.csproj` with `UndefineProperties="RuntimeIdentifier;RuntimeIdentifiers"` so MSBuild does not propagate the test-host RID into the production project (which would make Bible.Alarm try to write `runtimeconfig.json` into a per-RID subdirectory the MAUI targets don't pre-create — `GenerateRuntimeConfigurationFiles` then throws `DirectoryNotFoundException`).

### `<IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>` on the device-test hosts

`Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj` and `Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj` both set `<IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>`. **This is intentional.** Their tests run via `adb` (Android, against `TestRunnerActivity`) or `xharness apple test` (iOS) against the built APK/.app, *never* via `dotnet test` / vstest. Without this opt-out, `dotnet test Bible.Alarm.sln` (used by both `test-windows` in CI and `tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform Windows`) tries to spawn a `net10.0-android` testhost process on the Windows runner and crashes with `You must install or update .NET to run this application` (Android testhost runtime is not installable on Windows). `<SonarQubeTestProject>true</SonarQubeTestProject>` is preserved so SonarCloud still classifies them as test code and excludes them from the production-coverage denominator.

If you ever flip `IsTestProject` back to `true`, you must also remove the device-test hosts from the sln *or* invoke `dotnet test` with explicit per-project paths — the two are mutually exclusive given today's CI runners.

### Trim/linker disabled on the Android test host

`Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj` sets `<PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>`. **Required.** xunit discovers `[Fact]`/`[Theory]` methods at runtime via reflection, and the IL linker (active by default for MAUI Android Release builds) cannot see those references and strips the test methods. Symptoms when stripped: the activity launches, the entry point reports zero tests, `TestResults.xml` is empty, `done.txt` reads `0` (no failures because nothing ran). The trade-off is fine: the test APK is never shipped to users, so APK size doesn't matter.

**Do not** add `<AndroidLinkMode>None</AndroidLinkMode>` or `<TrimMode>copy</TrimMode>` alongside `<PublishTrimmed>false>`. The first is a legacy Xamarin property that runs *in addition to* the modern IL linker and corrupts the ACW typemap — `MainApplication.n_onCreate` then has no JNI binding and the test process dies with `UnsatisfiedLinkError: ... crc64e6b23cf938c82f06.MainApplication.n_onCreate` at app launch. `<PublishTrimmed>false>` alone disables trimming cleanly; let the Android tooling generate the typemap normally.

### UI integration tests (Appium) — separate axis, separate gate

`tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/` is **not** a coverage project. It targets `net10.0` and references **only** `Appium.WebDriver` (no `Bible.Alarm` project ref). Tests drive the *deployed* production app via the Appium 2 WebDriver protocol:

- Windows → Appium `windows` driver → WinAppDriver (legacy, archived in 2024 but still the only stable Windows option; we accept this trade-off because Windows cannot be excluded).
- Android → Appium `uiautomator2` driver.
- iOS    → Appium `xcuitest` driver (runs on a networked Mac via SSH, like the device-test pass).

Why a separate project (instead of folding UI tests into `Bible.Alarm.Tests`):

1. **Different runtime contract.** Coverage tests instrument production assemblies and run *in the same process* as the SUT. Appium tests run *out-of-process* and exercise a deployed binary — coverlet has no way to instrument across that boundary, so they produce zero coverage XML.
2. **Different prerequisites.** Coverage tests need only `dotnet test`. UI tests need a running Appium server, the platform driver, the deployed app, and (on Windows) a discoverable AUMID. None of those are ever true on a naive `dotnet test Bible.Alarm.sln`.
3. **Different flakiness profile.** UI tests time out on slow simulator boots, splash screens, animation frames. Mixing them into the coverage gate would block SonarCloud rollups on cosmetic glitches.

How `dotnet test Bible.Alarm.sln` skips them: every UI test class carries `[Trait("UI","<Windows|Android|iOS>")]`. The coverage filter in `tests/run-tests.ps1` (and `.github/workflows/build.yml` `test-windows`) is:

```text
--filter "Platform!=Android&Platform!=iOS&UI!=Windows&UI!=Android&UI!=iOS"
```

UI tests opt INTO execution via `--filter "UI=Windows"` (or `=Android`/`=iOS`), driven by `tests/run-ui-tests.ps1`. That orchestrator: starts Appium 2 on `127.0.0.1:4723`, deploys/installs the production app, discovers the Windows AUMID via `Get-StartApps`, sets the env vars the fixtures read (`APPIUM_URL`, `BIBLE_ALARM_AUMID`, etc.), runs the trait-filtered slice, and tears Appium down.

Fixture pattern (in `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/Fixtures/`):

- `AppiumFixtureBase` owns the lifecycle (xunit `IAsyncLifetime`), the `WaitForAccessibilityId` helper, and screenshot-on-failure dump. Tests reach the helper through the public surface — keep it public; the fixtures are themselves test-only types so widening here costs nothing.
- `WindowsAppiumFixture` / `AndroidAppiumFixture` / `IosAppiumFixture` build the platform-specific `AppiumOptions` and construct the right driver (`WindowsDriver` / `AndroidDriver` / `IOSDriver`).

Test pattern: each smoke test class is `IClassFixture<TFixture>`, has `[Trait("UI","<platform>")]`, and asserts on `_fixture.WaitForAccessibilityId("<AutomationId>")`. The home-page smokes anchor on `AutomationId="HomePage"` set in `src/Bible.Alarm/Views/Home.xaml`. **When you write a new UI test, prefer adding an `AutomationId` to the production XAML over relying on XPath/text-based queries — XPath queries in WinAppDriver are O(n) over the entire tree and brittle across MAUI versions.**

Local install steps (Windows host, one-time): `npm i -g appium@2`; `appium driver install --source=npm appium-windows-driver`; `appium driver install uiautomator2`; `winget install Microsoft.WinAppDriver`. Full guide: [`tests/UITESTS.md`](../../../tests/UITESTS.md).

CI today: UI tests build but do **not** run in any GitHub Actions job. Wiring `ui-windows` / `ui-android` / `ui-ios` jobs (each starting Appium on the runner before invoking `dotnet test --filter UI=<platform>`) is a follow-up. Do not gate `sonar` on those jobs when added — keep the coverage pass cleanly separate.

### Why no Instrumentation on Android

The Android host (`tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android/`) intentionally ships **only** `Platforms/Android/AndroidManifest.xml` and `Platforms/Android/TestRunnerActivity.cs`. There is no `MainApplication`, no `MainActivity`, no `MauiProgram`, no `App.xaml`, and **no `[Instrumentation]`-attributed class**. Reason hierarchy:

1. **No `[Application]` class** because the test APK references `Bible.Alarm.csproj`, which contributes its own `[Application]`-attributed `Bible.Alarm.Platforms.Android.MainApplication`. A second one trips Xamarin.Android's manifest merger with `XAGMM7009: There can be only one type with an [Application] attribute`. So we inherit the production `MainApplication` (which boots the real MAUI app, DI graph, MediaSession, etc.) — exactly what tests want anyway.

2. **No `[Instrumentation]` class** because of how Mono initializes on Android. Android's `ActivityThread.handleBindApplication` instantiates the Instrumentation class via JNI **before** any managed code from `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.dll` is touched. The Java ACW declares `n_onCreate(Bundle)` as `native`; Xamarin.Android only registers that JNI binding when the corresponding C# type is loaded from managed code. With Instrumentation, no managed code has loaded the type yet, so JNI lookup fails:

   ```
   java.lang.UnsatisfiedLinkError: No implementation found for void
     com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests.TestInstrumentation.n_onCreate(android.os.Bundle)
   ```

   The test process dies in ~5 seconds with no test output. Activities, by contrast, are constructed *after* `Application.OnCreate`, so Mono is fully up and `RegisterNatives` has wired the ACW. We therefore launch tests via:

   ```text
   adb shell am start -W -n com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests/com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests.TestRunnerActivity
   ```

3. **Device-side results contract.** `TestRunnerActivity.RunTestsAsync` always writes the following under `/sdcard/Documents/test-results/` before calling `Environment.Exit`:

   | File                                | Contents                                                              |
   | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   | `TestResults.xml`                   | xunit v2 XML produced by `DefaultAndroidEntryPoint`                   |
   | `coverage.android.opencover.xml`    | coverlet OpenCover, written by `AppDomain.ProcessExit` (fired by `Environment.Exit`) |
   | `done.txt`                          | ASCII integer return code (0 = success). The orchestrator polls for this. |
   | `error.txt`                         | Present **only** if `RunAsync` threw — full `Exception.ToString()`.   |

   The orchestrator (Windows: `tests/run-tests.ps1` → `Run-AndroidTests`; CI: `.github/workflows/build.yml` → `test-android`) `adb pull`s this directory, asserts `done.txt == "0"`, copies `coverage.android.opencover.xml` into the `coverage-android` artifact slot, then `adb uninstall`s the package.

The iOS host (`tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS/`) **does** keep its own `Program.cs` and `AppDelegate.cs`, but the AppDelegate is registered with `[Register("BibleAlarmTestsAppDelegate")]` — *not* `"AppDelegate"`. That ObjC class name has to differ from the one Bible.Alarm's production AppDelegate registers, otherwise the ObjC runtime sees two classes with the same name in the same process and rejects the bundle. `Program.Main` then explicitly wires UIKit to the test AppDelegate via `UIApplication.Main(args, null, typeof(AppDelegate))` (resolved in our namespace). Bible.Alarm's `Program.Main` is dead code in this build because only the entry assembly's `Main` is the executable entry point. iOS does **not** have the Mono-vs-Instrumentation race Android does — `xharness apple test` launches the test bundle as an ordinary app whose `AppDelegate.FinishedLaunching` runs after the runtime is up.

## Where does a new test go?

**Before writing the test, ask: "What does the production code under test compile against?"**

| Production code lives in...                                          | Test goes in...                                              | Trait required                                                                  |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `src/Bible.Alarm.Shared/**` (no MAUI dependency)                     | `tests/Bible.Alarm.Shared.Tests/<mirror-folder>/`            | none                                                                            |
| `src/Bible.Alarm/**` outside `Platforms/` (incl. ViewModels, Views, Services) | `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/<mirror-folder>/`          | none — runs on **all three** test jobs                                          |
| `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Windows/**` or `*.windows.cs`             | `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Platforms/Windows/<mirror-folder>/` | `[Trait("Platform","Windows")]`                                                 |
| `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Android/**` or `*.android.cs` or `#if ANDROID` | `tests/Platforms/Android/<mirror-folder>/`              | `[Trait("Platform","Android")]`                                                 |
| `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/iOS/**` or `*.ios.cs` or `#if IOS`        | `tests/Platforms/iOS/<mirror-folder>/`                       | `[Trait("Platform","iOS")]`                                                     |
| `.tools/<Tool>/**`                                                   | `tests/Bible.Alarm.<Tool>.Tests/<mirror-folder>/`            | none                                                                            |
| `libraries/<Library>/**`                                             | `tests/<Library>.Tests/<mirror-folder>/`                     | none                                                                            |
| Production UI flow exercised through the *deployed* app (no in-process state) | `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/<Smoke\|FlowName>/`         | `[Trait("UI","<Windows\|Android\|iOS>")]` per class — required, no exceptions   |

**Trait enforcement**: the Windows job runs with `--filter "Platform!=Android&Platform!=iOS"`. A platform-only test that forgets its trait will load on the Windows runner where its `using Bible.Alarm.Platforms.Android.*` references don't compile — so the failure is "this build, this file" instead of an opaque cross-job breakage. **Always tag platform tests.**

## Folder layout follows production code

Under each test project, mirror the production folder. Examples (use these as the pattern when adding new tests):

- Production `src/Bible.Alarm/Stores/ApplicationState.cs` → test `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Stores/ApplicationStateTests.cs`.
- Production `src/Bible.Alarm/ViewModels/HomeViewModelHelpers/HomeNavigationHelper.cs` → test `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/ViewModels/HomeViewModelHelpers/HomeNavigationHelperTests.cs`.
- Production `src/Bible.Alarm/Services/UI/ToastService.cs` → test `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Services/UI/ToastServiceTests.cs`.
- Platform-only `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Windows/Services/UI/WindowsNotificationIdGenerator.cs` → test `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Platforms/Windows/Services/UI/WindowsNotificationIdGeneratorTests.cs`.
- Platform-only `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Android/Services/Helpers/NotificationPermissionHelper.cs` → test `tests/Platforms/Android/NotificationPermissionHelperSmokeTests.cs`.

## Test-fixture style (xunit conventions used in this repo)

- `#nullable enable` at the top of every test file.
- `public sealed class FooTests { ... }` — sealed, no inheritance.
- `namespace Bible.Alarm.Tests;` (file-scoped). Platform-only tests under `tests/Platforms/{Android,iOS}/` use `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Platforms.{Android,iOS}`.
- `[Fact]` for single-shot tests, `[Theory]` + `[InlineData]` for parameterised.
- One assertion idea per `[Fact]`; method name reads like a sentence (`Constructor_accepts_schedules_and_overlay_flags`).
- Prefer **hand-rolled stub/recording subclasses** over Moq when the SUT exposes virtual methods (see `RecordingToastService` in `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Services/UI/ToastServiceTests.cs`). This repo does not use NSubstitute/Moq for new tests.
- Avoid `Task.Delay` and real I/O. If the SUT awaits time, inject an `Func<DateTime>` / `TimeProvider` and assert on synchronous state.
- Don't depend on a `MauiApp` host being initialised inside a test; the Android/iOS hosts boot one but the Windows host does not.

## How to invoke tests locally

The orchestrator script handles the boring parts (AVD boot, adb deploy + activity launch + done.txt poll for Android, SSH-to-Mac for iOS, coverage merge):

```pwsh
# Windows host-based pass (all net10.0 + net10.0-windows test projects). Filters out platform-only tests.
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform Windows

# Android device-test pass (boots an AVD if none is online; installs the APK, launches TestRunnerActivity,
# polls /sdcard/Documents/test-results/done.txt, pulls results + coverage, then uninstalls).
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform Android -AndroidAvd Pixel_8_API_34

# iOS device-test pass over SSH to a networked Mac (must have dotnet, maui-ios workload, Xcode, xharness)
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform iOS -MacHost user@mac.local

# All three, then merge into TestResults/merged/index.html via ReportGenerator
./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform All -MacHost user@mac.local
```

If you need to drop down to raw CLI (e.g. running a single test):

```pwsh
# Single-fixture filter via dotnet test (Windows pass)
dotnet test tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.csproj `
    --filter "FullyQualifiedName~ApplicationStateTests" `
    --logger "console;verbosity=detailed"

# Build + run an Android test APK manually (after dotnet workload install maui-android).
# CoverletOutput must be a *device* path — coverlet writes from the running app on /sdcard.
dotnet build tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj `
    -c Release -f net10.0-android -p:BUILD_ANDROID_ONLY=true `
    -p:CollectCoverage=true -p:CoverletOutputFormat=opencover `
    -p:CoverletOutput=/sdcard/Documents/test-results/coverage.android.opencover.xml

adb uninstall com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests
adb install -r tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android/bin/Release/net10.0-android/com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests-Signed.apk
adb shell rm -rf /sdcard/Documents/test-results
adb shell mkdir -p /sdcard/Documents/test-results
adb shell am start -W -n com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests/com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests.TestRunnerActivity

# Poll until /sdcard/Documents/test-results/done.txt appears, then pull and inspect
while (-not (adb shell 'if [ -f /sdcard/Documents/test-results/done.txt ]; then cat /sdcard/Documents/test-results/done.txt; fi')) { Start-Sleep 5 }
adb pull /sdcard/Documents/test-results/. TestResults/android/

# iOS equivalent — must run on macOS (maui-ios workload + Xcode)
dotnet build tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj \
    -c Debug -f net10.0-ios -p:BUILD_IOS_ONLY=true \
    -p:RuntimeIdentifier=iossimulator-arm64 \
    -p:CollectCoverage=true -p:CoverletOutputFormat=opencover \
    -p:CoverletOutput=TestResults/ios/coverage.ios.opencover.xml
dotnet xharness apple test \
    --app="$(find tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS/bin/Debug/net10.0-ios -maxdepth 3 -name '*.app' | head -n 1)" \
    --target=ios-simulator-64 \
    --output-directory=TestResults/ios
```

## How to invoke UI tests locally

`tests/run-ui-tests.ps1` is the dedicated orchestrator for the Appium-driven smoke tests in `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/`. It is **separate** from `run-tests.ps1` because UI tests require completely different prerequisites (running Appium server, deployed production app, platform-specific driver) and never contribute coverage. Full one-time install steps are in [`tests/UITESTS.md`](../../../tests/UITESTS.md); the commands below assume that's already done.

The script always: (1) builds + deploys the production app to the target platform (skip with `-SkipDeploy`), (2) starts a local Appium 2 server on `127.0.0.1:4723` (override with `-AppiumPort`), (3) sets the env vars the fixtures read (`APPIUM_URL`, `BIBLE_ALARM_AUMID` on Windows), (4) runs `dotnet test --filter UI=<platform>` against `Bible.Alarm.UITests`, and (5) tears Appium back down.

```pwsh
# Windows: deploys Bible.Alarm MSIX (Debug, win-x64) via dotnet build -t:Build,Deploy, auto-discovers
# AUMID via Get-StartApps, then drives WinAppDriver through the Appium 'windows' driver.
./tests/run-ui-tests.ps1 -Platform Windows
# Pass -Aumid '<AppUserModelId>' to override auto-discovery (rare; only when Get-StartApps misses
# the app). Pass -SkipDeploy to reuse an already-deployed MSIX (saves ~3 min on iteration).

# Android: deploys the production APK (release-signed Debug) to the currently-online emulator/device,
# then drives uiautomator2. Requires `adb devices` to show exactly one device. The script does NOT
# boot an AVD for you — start one yourself before invoking, or run `run-tests.ps1 -Platform Android`
# first to warm an emulator.
./tests/run-ui-tests.ps1 -Platform Android
./tests/run-ui-tests.ps1 -Platform Android -SkipDeploy   # if the app is already installed

# iOS: thin SSH driver. SSHes into the Mac and runs `tests/run-ui-tests-mac.sh`, which performs the
# build/deploy + Appium + xcuitest dance on the Mac. The repo must already be at ~/bible-alarm on
# the Mac — typically synced by a preceding `./tests/run-tests.ps1 -Platform iOS -MacHost ...` pass.
./tests/run-ui-tests.ps1 -Platform iOS -MacHost user@mac.local

# All three (Windows then Android then iOS, sequentially)
./tests/run-ui-tests.ps1 -Platform All -MacHost user@mac.local
```

When a UI test fails, screenshots and the Appium server log dump out to `%TEMP%/appium-ui-tests.log` (Windows) or `~/appium-ui-tests.log` (Mac). The fixture's `WaitForAccessibilityId` retries with exponential back-off and dumps a screenshot before throwing, so the failure mode is always "test threw with a PNG attached" rather than "test hung".

If you only want to run a single UI test (no Appium boot, no deploy — useful when iterating against an already-running Appium and an already-deployed app), bypass the orchestrator:

```pwsh
$env:APPIUM_URL = 'http://127.0.0.1:4723/'
$env:BIBLE_ALARM_AUMID = (Get-StartApps | ? Name -eq 'Bible Alarm').AppId   # Windows only
dotnet test tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/Bible.Alarm.UITests.csproj `
    --filter "UI=Windows&FullyQualifiedName~WindowsHomePageUITests" `
    --logger "console;verbosity=detailed"
```

UI tests do **not** run in any CI job today (see [UI integration tests (Appium)](#ui-integration-tests-appium--separate-axis-separate-gate)). When `ui-windows` / `ui-android` / `ui-ios` jobs are eventually wired up, they'll mirror the local script: install Appium 2 + the platform driver, deploy the app, then `dotnet test --filter UI=<platform>`. Do not gate `sonar` on those jobs — UI tests stay on a separate axis.

## Coverage feedback loop

After a successful run, OpenCover XML reports land at:

- Windows: `TestResults/<guid>/coverage.opencover.xml` (whatever folder coverlet.collector picked).
- Android: `TestResults/android/coverage.android.opencover.xml`.
- iOS: `TestResults/ios/coverage.ios.opencover.xml`.
- Merged HTML report: `TestResults/merged/index.html` (only after `-Platform All` or running `dotnet reportgenerator` manually against the three).

Open the merged HTML to see exactly which lines are uncovered. If working against an OpenCover XML by hand, search for `<SequencePoint vc="0"` to find uncovered lines (`vc` = visit count).

## SonarCloud coverage merge

The `sonar` CI job downloads the three artifacts (`coverage-windows`, `coverage-android`, `coverage-ios`) and feeds them via:

```
/d:sonar.cs.opencover.reportsPaths="TestResults/coverage-windows/coverage-windows.xml,TestResults/coverage-android/coverage-android.xml,TestResults/coverage-ios/coverage-ios.xml"
```

Sonar unions covered lines across all three reports — a line in `Services/Media/PlaybackService.cs` covered by either Windows tests *or* Android tests counts once. The new test host projects themselves are excluded from analysis via `sonar.exclusions="...,**/*.Tests.Android/**,**/*.Tests.iOS/**"` and `sonar.dotnet.excludeTestProjects="true"`.

## Hard rules — do not break

1. **Do not** add MAUI-runtime-dependent tests to `tests/Bible.Alarm.Shared.Tests/`. That project has no MAUI dependency by design.
2. **Do not** put files referencing `Bible.Alarm.Platforms.Android.*` or `Bible.Alarm.Platforms.iOS.*` types under `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/` outside `Platforms/Windows/`. The Android/iOS hosts exclude only `Platforms/**` from the cross-platform glob; everything else gets dragged into the APK/.app. References to types that don't exist for that TFM = build failure on that runner.
3. **Do not** remove the `BUILD_ANDROID_ONLY`, `BUILD_IOS_ONLY`, or `BUILD_WINDOWS_ONLY` switches in `src/Bible.Alarm/Bible.Alarm.csproj` and `libraries/CommunityToolkit.Maui.MediaElement/CommunityToolkit.Maui.MediaElement.csproj`. The Linux/macOS CI runners use them to skip the TFMs whose workloads they cannot install.
4. **Do not** remove the `dotnet-eng` feed entry or `packageSourceMapping` in repo-root `NuGet.config`. The `Microsoft.DotNet.XHarness.*` packages are not on `nuget.org`.
5. **Do not** rename `com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests` (Android `ApplicationId` + iOS `CFBundleIdentifier`) or the Android test activity name `com.jthomas.info.Bible.Alarm.Tests.TestRunnerActivity`. The orchestrator (and CI) launch tests via `adb shell am start -n <pkg>/<activity>`; rename either side without updating `tests/run-tests.ps1` + `.github/workflows/build.yml` and the run will fail with `Activity class {...} does not exist`.
   - Likewise on iOS, the `[Register("BibleAlarmTestsAppDelegate")]` name on `Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.AppDelegate` must stay distinct from Bible.Alarm's `[Register("AppDelegate")]`. Two classes with the same Objective-C name = ObjC runtime rejection at startup.
   - **Do not** add a `[Application]`-attributed `MainApplication`, a `[Activity(MainLauncher=true)]` `MainActivity`, or a `MauiProgram.cs` to `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android`. The test host inherits Bible.Alarm's `MainApplication` via project ref; declaring a second one trips `XAGMM7009` at manifest-merge time.
   - **Do not** add an `[Instrumentation]`-attributed class or `<instrumentation>` element to `AndroidManifest.xml`. Mono's JNI bindings are not registered before Android calls `Instrumentation.onCreate`, so the test process dies with `UnsatisfiedLinkError: ... TestInstrumentation.n_onCreate` before any tests execute. Tests run from `TestRunnerActivity` instead. (See [Why no Instrumentation on Android](#why-no-instrumentation-on-android).)
6. **Do not** swap `coverlet.collector` for `coverlet.msbuild` (or vice-versa) inside a project. Host-based tests use the collector (`--collect:"XPlat Code Coverage"`); device-test hosts use the msbuild instrumenter (`-p:CollectCoverage=true`) because the collector cannot inject into a running APK/.app.
   - On Android, `-p:CoverletOutput=...` must be a **device path** (`/sdcard/Documents/test-results/coverage.android.opencover.xml`). Coverlet's instrumented assembly resolves that path at runtime *on the emulator*, not on the build host. A Windows path silently no-ops on-device (the orchestrator then `adb pull`s the device file to `TestResults/android/`).
7. **Do not** flip `<IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>` to `true` in `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj` or `Bible.Alarm.Tests.iOS.csproj`. They must opt out of vstest discovery so `dotnet test Bible.Alarm.sln` skips them; the adb-driven Android flow and `xharness apple test` still find and run them via the built APK/.app. See the device-test hosts subsection above.
   - **Do not** flip `<PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>` back to `true` in `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj`. xunit needs reflection over `[Fact]`/`[Theory]` methods at runtime; trimming strips them and the activity ends up running zero tests with no error.
   - **Do not** add `<AndroidLinkMode>None</AndroidLinkMode>` or `<TrimMode>copy</TrimMode>` to `Bible.Alarm.Tests.Android.csproj` (a tempting "extra safety" addition). The legacy linker runs in parallel with the IL linker and corrupts the ACW typemap, breaking `MainApplication.n_onCreate` with `UnsatisfiedLinkError`. `<PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>` alone is sufficient.
8. **Do not** flatten the IDE solution folders by reassigning the 10 test-project GUIDs in `GlobalSection(NestedProjects)` in `Bible.Alarm.sln`. The five nesting folders (`tests`, plus `tests/{app,tools,libraries,ui}`) carry stable GUIDs `{F3E5C812-A100-4C76-8C1B-1A2B3C4D5E6{0,1,2,3,4}}`. If the IDE shows tests at the top level of the solution after a merge, those nesting rows got dropped — restore them from `git`.
9. **Do not** move `Bible.Alarm.sln` back into `src/`. Workflows (`.github/workflows/build.yml`, `deploy-windows.yml`, `deploy-ios.yml`) and `tests/run-tests.ps1` / `tests/run-ui-tests.ps1` all reference the root path; relocating the .sln is a wide-radius change that breaks CI silently (the `dotnet restore <path>` step succeeds against either location, but `dotnet build`/`dotnet test` on the wrong path will pick up a missing-projects build).
10. **Do not** drop any of the four trait clauses (`Platform!=Android`, `Platform!=iOS`, `UI!=Windows`, `UI!=Android`, `UI!=iOS`) from the `--filter` in `tests/run-tests.ps1` and `.github/workflows/build.yml`. The `Platform!=` pair excludes the device-test hosts' device-only tests (Android adb/Activity flow, iOS xharness); the three `UI!=` clauses exclude `Bible.Alarm.UITests` (which fails fast without an Appium server). Removing any clause turns a clean coverage pass into a hard failure on every CI run.
11. **Do not** add a `<ProjectReference>` to `Bible.Alarm.csproj` (or any production project) inside `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/Bible.Alarm.UITests.csproj`. UI tests must talk to the *deployed* app over WebDriver — coupling the test project to the production graph at compile time would let in-process state leak into "integration" tests and defeat the point of using Appium.
12. **Do not** remove `AutomationId="HomePage"` from `src/Bible.Alarm/Views/Home.xaml`. It is the anchor for `WindowsHomePageUITests`, `AndroidHomePageUITests`, and `IosHomePageUITests`. If you rename it, update all three smoke tests at once.

## Quick-reference paths to known-good exemplars

When writing a new test, copy the closest exemplar and adapt:

- **Plain xunit fact + `[Fact]`** → `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Stores/ApplicationStateTests.cs`
- **`[Theory]` + `[InlineData]`** → `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Common/Helpers/ConcurrencyHelperTests.cs`
- **Hand-rolled recording subclass** → `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Services/UI/ToastServiceTests.cs` (`RecordingToastService`)
- **Windows-only fixture (with trait)** → `tests/Bible.Alarm.Tests/Platforms/Windows/Services/UI/WindowsNotificationIdGeneratorTests.cs`
- **Android-only smoke test** → `tests/Platforms/Android/NotificationPermissionHelperSmokeTests.cs`
- **iOS-only smoke test** → `tests/Platforms/iOS/IosNotificationPermissionServiceSmokeTests.cs`
- **`net10.0` (Shared) fixture** → any file under `tests/Bible.Alarm.Shared.Tests/`
- **Appium UI smoke test (Windows/Android/iOS)** → `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/Smoke/{Windows,Android,Ios}HomePageUITests.cs` (each is a 6-line `[Fact]` that calls `_fixture.WaitForAccessibilityId(...)`)
- **Appium fixture pattern** → `tests/Bible.Alarm.UITests/Fixtures/AppiumFixtureBase.cs` + per-platform subclasses

For end-to-end context (CI fan-out, SDK pins, secrets), read `.github/workflows/build.yml` and `tests/README.md`. For UI test setup specifically, read `tests/UITESTS.md`.
